title: Tracking a folder
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: guide
summary: Set mf_watch on a directory, run a reconcile for what is already there, and the watcher follows it from then on; mf track records a single path.

Tracking a folder is two steps: say it is watched, then bring in what it already holds.

```
mf -n music metarecord -q 'mfr_path = "/albums"' field set mf_watch:bool=true
mf -n music reconcile
```

* `mf_watch = true` makes the directory and everything below it ''eligible''. The watcher starts following it at once — events there are recorded from now on.
* The files already present have no metarecord yet: a ''reconcile'' creates them ([[Running a reconcile]]) (and finds the files that moved while nobody watched).

To watch the whole repository, set `mf_watch` on the root (`mfr_path = ""`). A new
repository's root already carries the `default` ignore preset, so build output and
hidden entries are left out ([[Ignore presets]]); check what a path would do with
`mf watch check` or the file manager's markings ([[Eligibility]]).

In the GUI, select the directory and run `metarecord:reconcile watch`: it sets
`mf_watch = true` and reconciles that subtree in one gesture ([[Scoped reconcile]]).

!! Recording one path without watching

```
mf -n music track ./albums/new.flac      # prints its uuid
```

`mf track` creates the metarecord of a single file or directory — and those of its
missing parent directories — with `mf_watch = false`, so it is recorded but not
followed; it ignores eligibility, deciding the watch scope is left to you. It is
idempotent: a path already tracked prints its existing uuid. A path outside the
repository is refused (exit code 1). The file manager's ''Add to DB'' button
(`file-manager:track`) does the same for the selected entries.

!! Stopping

Set `mf_watch = false` on the directory (or add an ignore pattern). Nothing is deleted:
the metarecords stay as they are, frozen ([[When watch or ignore changes]]).
